libdatachannel v0.24.5: an idle pool worker looks at the queue again by itself

MPL-2.0, like src/impl/threadpool.cpp above. Modification history
(NereusSDR): 2026-10-11, J.J. Boyd (KG4VCF), AI-assisted via Anthropic
Claude Code.

A worker with nothing to run waited on the pool's condition variable with no
time limit, so a queued task depended on one wake-up reaching a worker. On
macOS 27.0 a desktop window froze for hours with a peer connection's
certificate task queued and all 18 workers in that wait, and a test run of
this pool alone ended the same way after 36.9 million tasks, where one more
task pushed to the pool did not free it. Why the wake-up reaches no worker
is not established. Each idle worker now waits at most 100 ms before it
looks at the queue again, and no longer than that when a later timed task
is at the head. Task order, timed tasks, joining and the busy-worker count
are unchanged.

--- a/src/impl/threadpool.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/threadpool.cpp
@@ -11,6 +11,10 @@
 
 namespace rtc::impl {
 
+// NereusSDR: an idle worker looks at the queue again after this long, woken
+// or not, so a task whose wake-up never arrived is still taken.
+const auto IDLE_RECHECK_INTERVAL = std::chrono::milliseconds(100);
+
 ThreadPool &ThreadPool::Instance() {
 	static ThreadPool *instance = new ThreadPool;
 	return *instance;
@@ -86,10 +90,12 @@
 		--mBusyWorkers;
 		scope_guard guard([&]() { ++mBusyWorkers; });
 		mWaitingCondition.notify_all();
-		if (time)
-			mTasksCondition.wait_until(lock, *time);
-		else
-			mTasksCondition.wait(lock);
+		// NereusSDR: never wait on the notification alone. A wake-up that does
+		// not reach any worker must not leave a queued task waiting for ever.
+		auto deadline = clock::now() + IDLE_RECHECK_INTERVAL;
+		if (time && *time < deadline)
+			deadline = *time;
+		mTasksCondition.wait_until(lock, deadline);
 	}
 	return nullptr;
 }
